    {
      "id": "Ugz5q5c3ljmV-mkNj3h4AaABAg",
      "author": "@gwugluud",
      "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/PRXs90-BCXJfa5dyTy2Qyt_jZwYomEsa1xYtCc4H23SCcuO2PRiXdDqwQjotm9QQ9i-tS9Rv=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
      "text": "I’m with the peeps who chose to stay in Centralia PA. Provided I had normal utilities and good broadband, I would love it there, especially if I could score a house with no close-by neighbors. I prefer living in a thinly-populated place. I’ve tried living in labyrinthine crime-o-plex hellholes such as L.A. and Dallas before, where you live half your life sitting in traffic, and people try your doorknob at night. As for carbon monoxide, sulphur vapors etc eking from the ground there, and the possibility of any given spot collapsing etc, in CA, peeps casually live with the knowledge that “the big one” can happen at any moment, and in any major city, one must live with the hazards which the enrichment of diversity brings. The fumes dissipate upon leaving the ground in Centralia, and as long as none of that is happening right under my house, I’d pay it little mind.",
      "likeCount": 0,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-06T01:33:20Z",
      "replyCount": 1,
